// Q&A tables per segment number: { window, rows: [[question, answer, owner], ...] }
const QA = {
  1: { window: 'during the talk / 17:04–17:10', rows: [
    ['Why is Sprint 3 only “integrated” when the code was merged?', 'Our Definition of Done says "on dev" means shown on the deployed environment; a local run does not count. Checkout and scheduling were still off on dev at the freeze (#223), so the review recorded the goal as not met and carried it into Sprint 4. Evidence: sprint-03/review.md, sprint-03/plan.md §8.', 'Lim'],
    ['Are these hours actuals?', 'No. We planned in hours per story; done hours are planned hours of finished work (Sprint 2 report, Sprint 3 review), and the Sprint 4 burndown sums planned estimates of cards not yet Done. Actual hours per person were not logged; that is a limitation we state.', 'Lim'],
    ['Was the effort balanced across the five members?', 'The plans gave everyone 30 h in Sprints 2 and 3; Sprint 4 started at 38–51 h each and is being rebalanced (PR #538). Board cards differ in size, for example one Stripe pull request was about 10,000 lines, so card counts are not effort. {{CONFIRM:the effort split all five agree on}}', 'Lim'],
    ['The proposal promised JWT. Where is that change recorded?', 'In the locked backend decisions of 30 Aug (backend-skeleton-plan.md D6/D7: Sa-Token session in Redis, RBAC from SQL tables) and frontend ADR 0002 (same-origin /api, HttpOnly cookie). Reason: a server session can be revoked and keeps the token out of JavaScript. Limitation: there is no numbered backend ADR for it (open item 7).', 'Lim → Liu Jin'],
    ['Why did you build an admin GUI when the proposal excluded it?', 'The exclusion was a merchant self-service GUI. Admins needed to configure team terms on real listings to make START possible on dev (US25, #221), and an API-only flow was error-prone in the demo; the console reuses the same versioned API (frontend ADR 0015). It is extra scope, not a replacement.', 'Lim'],
    ['What is your biggest open risk for the demo?', 'The Stripe webhook on dev (#517): payments stay waiting, so paid START, JOIN and refunds do not complete on dev yet. Go/no-go for the live payment steps is 23 Oct, with backup recordings by 24 Oct.', 'Lim → Yorik'],
  ] },
  2: { window: 'during the talk / 17:04–17:10', rows: [
    ['Why a monolith on dev and services on prod?', 'Decision: one codebase, two topologies (backend ADR 0008). Reason: SWE5006 grades a complete runnable system, so dev stays the monolith on one EC2 host; SWE5001 needs three services on ECS. The services are thin assemblies of the same modules, and ArchUnit holds the boundaries in the monolith too.', 'Eric'],
    ['Is ProductStatus really the State pattern, or an enum switch?', 'State, with enum constants as the concrete states: each constant with a body compiles to its own subclass, overrides only the moves it allows and returns the next state; the defaults refuse with 409, and Product delegates without branching on status. Evidence: ProductStatus.java, ProductStatusTest. GroupDeal uses a transition table (DealStatus.canTransitionTo), which we do not call State.', 'Liu Jin'],
    ['How do you stop a double refund?', 'A durable obligation, then an idempotent call. The obligation is unique per payment and commits with the expiry; the Stripe idempotency key is built from its ID; a lease plus a version fence stops two workers; after 23 hours the worker only searches Stripe, never creates. Evidence: RefundServiceTest.lostResponseRetriesSameDurableKey. Limitation: proven locally with a provider substitute, not on dev (#223).', 'Yorik'],
    ['Why a fixed 30 s retry and not exponential backoff?', 'A fixed delay in RefundMapper.xml: the idempotency key makes each retry safe, and the 23-hour cutoff ends automatic retries. There is no exponential backoff or jitter, so we call it a fixed delay.', 'Yorik'],
    ['Why a Factory Method with only one creator?', 'The creator is a seam: initiate is final, so any creator keeps the validation, and constructor injection (#421) lets a test or configuration supply another creator without editing the service. Limitation: one creator, chosen at build time; we do not claim runtime-pluggable creation.', 'Eric'],
    ['Is there a database per service?', 'On dev: one PostgreSQL database, schema "domain", all tables. On ECS: one RDS instance with a database per service, and each runtime role is granted only its own tables (ADR 0008). Full list: B4.', 'Liu Jin'],
    ['Do notifications work on dev?', 'No, by decision: the dev host runs no broker and leaves the outbox writer, relay and consumer off (application-ec2.yml; ADR 0007 scope decision, 11 Oct). The path runs on ECS through SNS and SQS, and locally and in CI against ElasticMQ (OutboxRelayElasticMqIT, NotificationConsumerElasticMqIT).', 'Pan'],
  ] },
  3: { window: 'during the talk / 17:04–17:10', rows: [
    ['How do you test payments without calling Stripe?', 'Stripe sits behind gateway ports (Checkout and Refund gateways). Unit tests swap the port; StripeRolloutAcceptanceIT (8 tests) runs the real HTTP endpoints, scheduler, Flyway schema and webhook signature checks, with only OAuth and the Stripe network calls substituted. Limitation: no live Sandbox refund on dev yet (#223, #517).', 'Liu Jin → Yorik'],
    ['Why Testcontainers rather than H2?', 'Real PostgreSQL 18, Redis 8.10 and ElasticMQ in containers. Our invariants live in the database: UNIQUE keys, ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING, guarded UPDATEs, role grants; H2 would accept or reject different SQL. Evidence: OrderRepositoryIT.duplicatePaymentIsRejectedAtTheDatabase. Cost: Docker is needed and the backend build takes about 13 minutes.', 'Liu Jin'],
    ['Do you do TDD?', 'Not for everything, and we say so. Test-first for selected changes: the rate-limit checks failed (429 expected, 200 actual) before the code (3 Oct log); two red tests reproduced the join-quote defect before the fix (7 Oct log). Elsewhere tests come in the same pull request, and the 80% new-code gate makes them mandatory.', 'Liu Jin'],
    ['What is your test coverage?', 'SonarCloud on dev, read 19 Oct: backend 99.4% overall and 99.2% on new code; frontend 96.1% overall and 96.2% on new code. The gate is at least 80% on new code (NFR-06). Limitation: coverage is not assertion quality, and we have no automated mutation testing. {{VERIFY:re-read SonarCloud on 25 Oct}}', 'Liu Jin'],
    ['How do you test the frontend against the API?', 'Contract first. The backend regenerates contracts/openapi.json and fails on drift; openapi-diff fails a breaking change unless labelled. The frontend generates its client and its MSW handlers from that file (frontend ADRs 0003, 0011), and an unhandled request fails a test. Limitation: stubs prove the frontend half only.', 'Liu Jin → Lim'],
    ['Why is the performance test not on dev?', 'Dev users exist only through Google sign-in, so 100 authenticated sessions cannot be scripted there, and dev is one host shared with its database. The scripted k6 test runs on the SWE5001 ECS environment, the same code (slide 24, run in §5); the Node workload ran locally on 30 Sep (docs/perf/sprint3-baseline.md). Neither is a dev result, and each is labelled.', 'Liu Jin'],
    ['Is 200 requests a second the same as 100 concurrent users?', 'Not directly, so we convert. k6 drives an open arrival rate; the proposal counts users. Little\'s law N = X(R + Z): 100 users thinking 0.5 s with about 30 ms responses send about 190 requests/s, inside the 200 we drive, and our gate is p95 < 500 ms, four times stricter than 2 s. The 0.5 s think time is our assumption. The local closed-loop run (30 Sep) used exactly 100 users with 200 ms think: p95 at most 57 ms.', 'Liu Jin'],
    ['Is the last-seat race test a unit test?', 'By name only. StripeCheckoutFlowTest.concurrentPaidBuyersCannotBothTakeLastSeat runs two threads against real PostgreSQL and MyBatis with the payment provider doubled, so by level it is an integration test. The pure unit tests of the same rule are PaidGroupDealTest (the aggregate\'s guards) and DealStatusTest (the transition table).', 'Liu Jin'],
    ['Why no end-user acceptance testing?', 'Our quadrant three is manual checks by the team, logged: the Stripe Sandbox browser acceptance (20 Sep, local) and sign-in cookie checks on dev (8 Oct). We had no access to outside users within the course, and we do not claim UAT.', 'Liu Jin'],
  ] },
  4: { window: 'during the talk / 17:04–17:10', rows: [
    ['Why EC2 and not ECS for dev?', 'Dev reuses one existing EC2 host with the monolith in Docker Compose (operator guide; backend ADR 0008): SWE5006 needs one complete, always-on system at low cost, and the monolith fits one t3.large. Limitation: one host, so a deploy restarts the API (502 on 3–4 Oct), and PostgreSQL and Redis share the host outside the pipeline.', 'Pan'],
    ['How do you roll back?', 'Automatic: deploy.sh health-checks the new container and restarts the previous digest if it fails; the workflow also checks public health. Manual: rollback-backend.yml (reason, confirmation, same concurrency group). Limitation: rollback-backend.yml has never run and no automatic rollback has fired. Flyway is never reversed, so migrations stay backward-compatible.', 'Pan'],
    ['How are secrets handled?', 'CI holds no AWS keys: OIDC gives short-lived roles per job. The repository has three Actions secrets, none for AWS or the app (SONAR_TOKEN, TYPESAFE_API_KEY, BOARD_SNAPSHOT_TOKEN; gh secret list, 19 Oct). Dev application secrets live in /etc/blocbuy/backend.env (root, 0600). Limitation: the account still has two IAM users for SSM port forwarding, one with an active key (governance README).', 'Pan'],
    ['Why is CodeQL off?', 'The job exists in security.yml behind ENABLE_CODEQL, which is false: code scanning is not enabled for this private repository (GitHub API, 19 Oct). SAST is covered by SonarCloud on every PR plus SpotBugs in the build. We list it as a gap (B9).', 'Pan'],
    ['How long from merge to live?', 'Medians from gh on 19 Oct: backend about 3.1 minutes from the push run starting to the deploy job ending (88 runs); frontend about 2.7 minutes including the e2e-dev smoke (94 runs). PR checks take about 16 minutes for backend and 5 for frontend. Per-workflow table: B8.', 'Pan'],
    ['How do you manage dependencies?', 'Dependabot checks Maven, npm, Docker and GitHub Actions daily with a cooldown and grouped updates (SpotBugs engine and plugin move together since PR #580); majors of Spring, Java, React and TypeScript are ignored on purpose. Every update passes the same required checks. Trivy scans dependencies on change and weekly. Actions are pinned to commit SHAs.', 'Pan → Lim'],
    ['Is the deployed image the one you tested?', 'Not byte for byte. The squash merge and the strict up-to-date rule make the merged tree the tested tree, but package-dev rebuilds it with -DskipTests and publish-ecr-dev builds the delivery image again without a Trivy scan; ECR scans it on push. That falls short of "build once, promote"; frontend ADR 0004 still describes promotion (open item 18).', 'Pan'],
    ['Is this Continuous Delivery or Continuous Deployment?', 'To dev it is Continuous Deployment: after the code owner approves and the PR merges, nothing manual remains (EPAT 07 CI p34–35). SWE5006 has no production. SWE5001 production is Continuous Delivery: a promotion PR into prod needs a CODEOWNERS approval and three required checks.', 'Pan'],
    ['Why no DAST or SBOM?', 'Not built: no dynamic scan runs against the deployed site, and no SBOM is generated. Dependency risk is covered by Trivy on the lockfiles and Dependabot; both gaps are listed in B9.', 'Pan'],
  ] },
  5: { window: '17:04–17:10', rows: [
    ['Why is there no auto-scaling on dev?', 'Dev is one EC2 host running the monolith in Docker Compose, with PostgreSQL and Redis on the host (backend ADR 0008), so SWE5006 needs one complete, always-on system at low cost. Auto-scaling exists only in the SWE5001 topology on ECS (commerce 2–6 tasks on ALB requests per target, read 21 Oct), which we do not claim for SWE5006. Limitation: the dev host is a single point of failure.', 'Yorik → Pan'],
    ['Is the refund real?', 'A real Stripe Sandbox refund: the Refund API in test mode, so no money moves. Locally on 20 Sep, refund re_3UHbeV… reached succeeded and a recheck created no duplicate (B11). On dev: {{FILL:Sandbox refund id from the 24 Oct dry run, or say "not yet proven on dev" (#223, #517)}}.', 'Yorik'],
    ['What happens if Stripe is down?', 'The Checkout attempt is saved before Stripe is called, so a timeout leaves one recoverable attempt with a stable provider key, never a second payment (StripeCheckoutFlowTest). Refunds stay as durable obligations; the worker retries every 30 s with the same idempotency key, and after 23 hours it only searches Stripe and hands mismatches to a person.', 'Yorik'],
    ['How do you avoid a double payment?', 'A repeated Checkout request with the same buyer, intent and Idempotency-Key reuses the payment. Webhook events are stored by event ID, so a replay changes nothing (StripeLocalIntegrationTest.eightConcurrentWebhookReplaysStillProduceOneMemberAndOneOrder). When two paid buyers race for the last seat, one gets it and the other gets a refund obligation (StripeCheckoutFlowTest.concurrentPaidBuyersCannotBothTakeLastSeat).', 'Yorik → Eric'],
    ['Why did the demo not show notifications?', 'By decision: the dev host runs no message broker, so the outbox writer, relay and consumer are off (application-ec2.yml; ADR 0007 scope decision, 11 Oct). Notifications run on the SWE5001 environment through SNS and SQS, and locally and in CI against ElasticMQ; slide 18 shows the relay’s Template Method.', 'Pan'],
    ['Why not race two buyers for the last seat on dev?', 'Because a live race is not repeatable on shared dev and would leave a refund obligation on a real account. The race is a test that runs on every pull request: twenty paid buyers race for the last seat of a team of two through the production intent and fulfilment ports against PostgreSQL 18 in Testcontainers; the team ends with 2 members, 2 orders, current_size 2 and 19 refund obligations (StripeLocalIntegrationTest.twentyBuyersRaceForLastSeatAndNineteenRefundObligationsPersist). It is a local test, and we say so (step D6).', 'Yorik → Eric'],
    ['How do you know expiry happens within one minute on dev?', 'By design and by a local measurement, not by a dev timestamp. TeamExpirySweepJob runs every minute (fixedDelay PT1M) under a database lease and expires every open team whose deadline has passed; the local run of 30 Sep measured a worst lag of 58.18 s for 30 teams under 100-user load. On dev the demo shows the outcome: the 16:05 bloc, due ≈ 16:45, is refunded by the time we look. {{VERIFY:whether the bloc row or receipt shows the deadline and the closing time on dev}}', 'Yorik'],
    ['Is 401 vs 403 vs 400 deliberate?', 'Yes. The Sa-Token interceptor checks login on every /api/v1 path except anonymous GETs of listings and invites, so no session is 401 COMMON_401_001; a signed-in buyer on an admin endpoint fails StpUtil.checkRole(ADMIN) and gets 403 COMMON_403_001; a money write without the X-BlocBuy-Request marker is 400 COMMON_400_001, and a wrong value is 403 (StripeCheckoutController.checkCsrf). Mapping: GlobalExceptionHandler.', 'Yorik → Liu Jin'],
    ['(Questions parked during the talk)', 'Lim reads each parked question from the backchannel in one line and names the owner; those come first. Answer each in 30–45 s: decision, reason, evidence or limitation.', 'Lim'],
  ] },
};

